=== Vendor tagger bug (important, not fixed — out of scope) ===
src/normalize.py:match_vendors() does a raw case-insensitive substring match
of each configured vendor token against the full text, with no word
boundaries. Confirmed false positives during this run:
  - "SEC" matches inside "security", "San Francisco" (!), etc. — appeared on
    the large majority of items regardless of actual SEC-8-K relevance.
  - "SSE" matches inside ordinary words.
  - "AWS" matched inside "flaws" (item 12, item 15) — no actual AWS mention
    in either article.
  - "Cisco" matched inside "San Francisco" (item 40, "Russian national
    facing 20 years..." — a Ktulaev malware-campaign sentencing story with
    no real Cisco connection; the tag is spurious).
This makes the vendor list on inbox items unreliable as-is, and it also
undermines automated clustering rule 4 ("shared vendor token AND within
24h") when applied literally / transitively — a naive union-find pass over
rule 4 produced garbage mega-clusters (e.g. merged the SonicWall/CISA KEV
story with the Cisco patch story and an unrelated sentencing story, purely
via "SEC"/"AWS"/"Cisco" substring collisions). Recommend switching
match_vendors() to a word-boundary regex per vendor token. Did not fix per
scope discipline — flagging for the user to action.

Because of this, clustering and vendor-match scoring below were done by
reading full article text for every candidate that mattered, not by trusting
the vendor tag field alone. Two vendor+time (rule 4) merges were manually
verified against full text and applied:
  - SonicWall SMA 1000 zero-day (darkreading, item 20) + CISA KEV addition
    covering the same SonicWall SMA 1000 SSRF flaw (thehackernews, item 12,
    CVE-2026-83548) — confirmed same vendor/product family, ~8.6h apart.
  - CrowdStrike Fal.Con 2026 conference: 7 YouTube posts from the
    `crowdstrike` channel (items 80-86) editorially grouped as one story
    (same live event, day 2) even though they don't share a CVE/URL and
    titles don't fuzzy-match >=85 against each other — this is an editorial
    grouping decision, not a mechanical cluster, made because rule 4 can't be
    trusted here (no reliable per-video vendor tag) and treating 7 separate
    Fal.Con video posts as 7 unrelated "clusters" would be a worse read of
    the actual news (one vendor conference, one story).

